text:
This frame of bones and skin,
weary at last of carrying a mad head about,
has had enough, and I am not surprised;
for though it is true that I am not old,

of the share of life allotted me
in the life of the world, to my own harm
I have made such use that I could swear
I have packed a century into every day.

And so, even if I died now,
I could not say I had not lived;
for the coat, though it looks new outside,
I know has grown old within.

Old it has grown, yes, curse my star!
My aching restlessness tells it well enough;
for some grief, as it passes, stamps
its dreadful mark on the heart, if not the brow.
